C = [
("h1", "9. The Two Women and the Silenced Voice"),
("p", "Claim: Revelation sets two women side by side, and it introduces them in the same words. One is the great whore of chapter 17, and the other is the bride, the Lamb’s wife, of chapter 21. The book also takes the prophets’ phrase for the voice of the bridegroom and the bride, which Jeremiah said would cease and then be heard again, and says in chapter 18 that it shall be heard no more in the city that has been judged. This chapter reads the contrast. It does not decide who the harlot is. That is a question for a volume on the judgments of the book, and the argument here needs only that there are two women and that the text keeps them apart."),
("sub", "9.1 One angel, one formula"),
("p", "The two visions open alike. In chapter 17 “there came one of the seven angels which had the seven vials, and talked with me, saying unto me, Come hither; I will shew unto thee the judgment of the great whore that sitteth upon many waters” (Revelation 17:1). In chapter 21 “there came unto me one of the seven angels which had the seven vials full of the seven last plagues, and talked with me, saying, Come hither, I will shew thee the bride, the Lamb’s wife” (Revelation 21:9). The angel is of the same company, the invitation is the same, and the thing shown is a woman in each case. The writer has built the second vision to be read against the first."),
("p", "The settings differ. Of the first the writer says, “So he carried me away in the spirit into the wilderness: and I saw a woman sit upon a scarlet coloured beast” (Revelation 17:3). Of the second, “he carried me away in the spirit to a great and high mountain, and shewed me that great city, the holy Jerusalem, descending out of heaven from God” (Revelation 21:10). A wilderness and a mountain, a beast and a descent from God, are set against each other on purpose."),
("sub", "9.2 Dress and name"),
("p", "The first woman “was arrayed in purple and scarlet colour, and decked with gold and precious stones and pearls, having a golden cup in her hand full of abominations and filthiness of her fornication” (Revelation 17:4). The second is given “fine linen, clean and white” (Revelation 19:8). The first has a name written on her forehead: “MYSTERY, BABYLON THE GREAT, THE MOTHER OF HARLOTS AND ABOMINATIONS OF THE EARTH” (Revelation 17:5). The second is shown as a city whose gates carry “the names of the twelve tribes of the children of Israel” (Revelation 21:12). One woman is named by what she has done among the nations, and the other is named by the people she belongs to."),
("p", "The first is “drunken with the blood of the saints, and with the blood of the martyrs of Jesus” (Revelation 17:6), and in the closing verse of chapter 18 “in her was found the blood of prophets, and of saints, and of all that were slain upon the earth” (Revelation 18:24). The second has the glory of God and a light like a stone most precious (Revelation 21:11). The reader is meant to see a harlot and a bride, and the book gives no sign of a woman who is both at once."),
("tbl", "The table sets the two visions side by side.", [
    ("Rev 17:1", "“Come hither; I will shew unto thee the judgment of the great whore”", "The angel opens the first vision"),
    ("Rev 21:9", "“Come hither, I will shew thee the bride, the Lamb’s wife”", "The same angel opens the second"),
    ("Rev 17:3", "“into the wilderness: and I saw a woman sit upon a scarlet coloured beast”", "The first woman is seen in a wilderness"),
    ("Rev 21:10", "“to a great and high mountain”", "The second is seen from a mountain"),
    ("Rev 17:4", "“arrayed in purple and scarlet colour”", "The harlot’s dress"),
    ("Rev 19:8", "“fine linen, clean and white”", "The wife’s dress"),
    ("Rev 17:18", "“that great city, which reigneth over the kings of the earth”", "The first woman is a city that reigns"),
    ("Rev 21:10", "“that great city, the holy Jerusalem, descending out of heaven from God”", "The second is a city that comes from God"),
  ], [0.13, 0.55, 0.32]),
("sub", "9.3 Two cities"),
("p", "Each woman is a city. The angel says of the first, “the woman which thou sawest is that great city, which reigneth over the kings of the earth” (Revelation 17:18). The bride is shown as “that great city, the holy Jerusalem” (Revelation 21:10). The same figure is carried by both. That is a reason to read the bride as a city of people rather than as a company of individuals brought to a husband, and it is the reading that the gates and foundations of chapter 21 support. The names of the tribes stand on the gates, and the city is the place where Israel’s people dwell."),
("sub", "9.4 The voice that is taken and given back"),
("p", "Jeremiah spoke of the bridal voice three times as a thing the LORD would remove. “Then will I cause to cease from the cities of Judah, and from the streets of Jerusalem, the voice of mirth, and the voice of gladness, the voice of the bridegroom, and the voice of the bride: for the land shall be desolate” (Jeremiah 7:34). The same phrase appears again at 16:9, and at 25:10 it is joined with “the sound of the millstones, and the light of the candle.” It is a mark of desolation. When the voice is silent, the marriage is, in the figure, over."),
("p", "Then he promised that it would return. “Again there shall be heard in this place … the voice of the bridegroom, and the voice of the bride … For I will cause to return the captivity of the land, as at the first, saith the LORD” (Jeremiah 33:10–11). The voice is taken from Jerusalem and given back to Jerusalem."),
("p", "Revelation takes these words into the judgment of the city of chapter 18. “The voice of harpers, and musicians, and of pipers, and trumpeters, shall be heard no more at all in thee; … and the sound of a millstone shall be heard no more at all in thee; And the light of a candle shall shine no more at all in thee; and the voice of the bridegroom and of the bride shall be heard no more at all in thee” (Revelation 18:22–23). The sound of the millstone, the light of the candle and the voice of the bridegroom and the bride are the three marks of Jeremiah 25:10, and here they are said to cease in Babylon. The book speaks in the prophets’ own language. It uses the language of the silenced marriage for the city that is judged, and it uses the language of the restored marriage for the city that comes down from God."),
("p", "The bride’s voice is not left silent. In the closing verses of the book “the Spirit and the bride say, Come” (Revelation 22:17). The voice that Jeremiah said would be heard again in Jerusalem is heard in the last chapter of the book. The thread of the marriage figure, which began with the wife’s unfaithfulness and the husband’s anger, ends with the wife speaking."),
("sub", "9.5 What the contrast shows"),
("p", "Three things follow. The first is that the book itself distinguishes two women, so a reader who merges the bride with another company must find the merging in the text and not in the tradition. The second is that the bride is described with the prophets’ words and the harlot with the prophets’ words, so that the whole contrast is built from Israel’s own scripture, and the wife of chapter 21 is the wife who was promised in Jeremiah and Isaiah. The third is that Paul’s letters, which use the marriage pattern to teach the Body, are not part of this web of allusions. They do not speak of a harlot, of a city that reigns, of a voice that ceases, or of a gate with a tribe’s name. Where the vocabulary is the prophets’, the figure is the prophets’."),
("sub", "9.6 What this chapter does not say"),
("bul", [
    "It does not say who the harlot of chapter 17 is. The chapter holds only that she is one woman and the bride is another, and that the book tells them apart.",
    "It does not say that Revelation 18:23 is a quotation of Jeremiah. It says that the phrases are the same phrases, and that the book writes in the language of the prophets.",
    "It does not say that the voice of the bride in Revelation 22:17 can be named by the verse alone. It says that the voice is heard, and that the thread of the figure ends there.",
  ]),
("call", "Where the argument stands", "Revelation introduces the harlot and the bride with the same angel and the same invitation, and sets them as two women and two cities. The silenced voice of Jeremiah is silent in the judged city and heard again in the book’s last chapter. The contrast is built from the prophets’ own words."),
]
